+A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names, so it never combines field edits itself; a visit changes only as a whole.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+
+### Submission envelope
+
+Each submission carries:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| visit ID | Client-generated UUID of the visit |
+| base revision ID | The revision the edit started from; absent when the submission creates the visit |
+| payload | The complete visit: plot reference, observation date, observers, species cover estimates, notes |
+| idempotency key | Makes retries safe |
+
+The server assigns a revision ID and receipt time on acceptance. The author comes from authentication, not the payload. A base revision ID that does not name an accepted revision of the same visit is rejected.
+
+### Revisions and heads
+
+An accepted submission becomes a **revision** of its visit whose parent is the base revision. A revision with no accepted child is a **head**. Heads are determined only by base revision IDs, never by receipt order.
+
+- One head: that revision is the visit's current state.
+- More than one head: the visit has unresolved review work, and its heads are the competing revisions.
+
+A submission whose base is not a head, because another accepted revision already derives from it, becomes an additional head. A submission whose base is one of several competing heads extends that head; the others remain competing.
+
+### Current-state response
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+Retrieval returns one of two shapes:
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+- **Current**: the single head's revision, payload, author, and receipt time.
+- **Unresolved**: every competing head with its payload, author, receipt time, and base revision ID, plus the most recent resolved revision when one exists. No competing head is presented as current.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+### Resolution
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+A coordinator resolves an unresolved visit by naming the complete set of heads being resolved and supplying the resulting complete visit payload, which may equal one head's payload. Acceptance creates a new revision whose parents are all named heads, recorded with the coordinator's identity and the contributing submission IDs. If a new head arrived after the coordinator's view, the named set no longer matches the visit's heads and the resolution is rejected so the coordinator reviews the new head.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent visit edits | Whole-visit revisions: each submission names its base revision; edits from the same base compete and go to a coordinator | Field-aware three-way merge that combines disjoint field edits automatically and reviews overlaps | Merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries must be maintained, and can combine scientifically related edits without noticing. Whole-visit revisions keep a worker's submitted visit intact and accept a larger review queue, including review of unrelated edits. Tablets need only base revision IDs rather than base snapshots and structured changes. |
+| Meaning of "current" | Derived from the revision graph (heads), not from receipt time | Latest-received wins | Arrival order is not evidence of authority; late-arriving offline edits would otherwise silently override earlier ones. |
+| Unresolved visit state | No current state is reported while more than one head exists | Report the most recent resolved revision as current | A superseded state presented as current would let export carry a value that a submitted edit contradicts. The last resolved revision is still returned for reference. |
 
